Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Introduction", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9865523037224503`*^9, 
  3.986552305252205*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"4c5828e1-7c8f-41ec-8f82-\
48fd3420a5e2"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "Type~Ia supernovae (SNe~Ia) provide one of the most direct observational \
probes of the cosmic expansion history through the luminosity \
distance--redshift relation. Large compilations such as \
Pantheon+~\\cite{Brout2022}, building on earlier \
compilations~\\cite{Scolnic2018}, and constructed using standard light-curve \
fitting techniques such as SALT2~\\cite{Guy2007}, enable high-precision tests \
of cosmological models using covariance-weighted statistical methods. In the \
standard framework, Type~Ia supernova observations are interpreted within a \
parameterized expansion history specified by the Hubble constant and the \
relative contributions of matter, radiation, and dark energy, with spatial \
flatness often assumed. These parameters determine the redshift dependence of \
the expansion rate ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         StyleBox["H", "TI"], "(", 
         StyleBox["z", "TI"], ")"}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "$H(z)$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4c019090-a0f8-464c-b95b-e9e16853141d"],
 " and, through it, the luminosity distance."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.98655231839398*^9, 3.986552345096964*^9}, 
   3.9865549315401497`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"a3402ec9-6a65-ef4d-85ed-\
46e1341a079d"],

Cell["\<\
An alternative approach is to consider constrained analytic forms for the \
distance--redshift relation and to evaluate their performance directly \
against the data. In this work, we examine a model in which the distance \
relation is parameterized by a small set of quantities, one of which is an \
acceleration scale determined independently from galaxy dynamics. This \
approach enables a direct test of whether a single empirically determined \
scale can consistently describe both galactic and cosmological observables.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.986552328409292*^9, 
  3.986552343444063*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1ead8dc9-8593-ce42-a797-\
0f8f0692cda0"],

Cell["\<\
The acceleration scale is obtained from fits to the baryonic Tully--Fisher \
relation using the SPARC galaxy sample, yielding a fixed value that is not \
adjusted in the supernova analysis. The remaining parameter is calibrated \
using the Cepheid subset of the Pantheon+ sample, and the resulting model is \
evaluated on the out-of-sample supernovae using the full covariance matrix.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9865523390192013`*^9, 
  3.986552341958891*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"24ad3c07-b77e-5c46-9f0d-\
97afd9fc1e93"],

Cell["\<\
The goal of this paper is to assess whether this constrained analytic \
relation provides a quantitatively consistent description of the observed \
SNe~Ia distance moduli across redshift under identical calibration \
conditions. No assumption is made regarding the underlying gravitational or \
cosmological theory, and the analysis is restricted to the observables \
considered here.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.986552355091505*^9},ExpressionUUID->"7b9b0dd2-d51a-2f47-97e9-\
f76488d098e8"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Phenomenological Acceleration Law", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9766534147713966`*^9, 3.9766534257402344`*^9}, {
   3.9766535822523575`*^9, 3.9766535874466724`*^9}, 3.976903850756321*^9, 
   3.9810190573806267`*^9, 3.981019187016672*^9, 
   3.986552369988041*^9},ExpressionUUID->"1678be23-cfac-41af-b548-\
80744f4116fa"],

Cell["\<\
The purpose of this work is to test whether a single empirically determined \
acceleration scale can link two otherwise distinct observational regimes: \
circular motion in rotationally supported galaxies and the luminosity \
distances of Type~Ia supernovae. We define a phenomenological acceleration \
law for circular orbits, determine the acceleration scale $A$ from the \
baryonic Tully--Fisher relation, and then hold it fixed in the supernova \
analysis. No underlying gravitational theory is assumed. This separation is \
essential: the acceleration scale entering the distance--redshift relation is \
not fitted to the supernova sample.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.976903863153343*^9, 3.9769038698008175`*^9}, {
   3.9769040644038124`*^9, 3.9769040866725273`*^9}, {3.9769083342357635`*^9, 
   3.9769083360260315`*^9}, 3.97713818960483*^9, 3.9800129020364227`*^9, 
   3.9800129753290443`*^9, 3.980013047956032*^9, {3.9800135067017536`*^9, 
   3.980013507206949*^9}, 
   3.9865527673295174`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"4aac8d4c-2aee-e04d-8b81-\
df482f4c2bf5"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Circular Orbits", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975607595405895*^9, 3.975607618234625*^9}, 
   3.9769041312922554`*^9, 3.977256704842144*^9, 
   3.9865527874756756`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"b31bca59-6b7c-4094-a5e1-\
b1688c638111"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "We consider circular motion in an acceleration field consisting of a \
Newtonian component and a constant inward term ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       StyleBox["A", "TI"], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "$A$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "be1648f1-4b7c-e54d-aa2d-1634fdd0976f"],
 ". For a test body in a circular orbit of radius ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       StyleBox["r", "TI"], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, "input" -> 
     "$r$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "5813adcb-747e-5345-8593-8bf0b9e8c609"],
 " about an enclosed baryonic mass ",
 Cell[BoxData[
  FormBox[
   TemplateBox[<|"boxes" -> FormBox[
       RowBox[{
         SubscriptBox[
          StyleBox["M", "TI"], 
          StyleBox["b", "TI"]], "(", 
         StyleBox["r", "TI"], ")"}], TraditionalForm], "errors" -> {}, 
     "input" -> "$M_b(r)$", "state" -> "Boxes"|>,
    "TeXAssistantTemplate"], TraditionalForm]],ExpressionUUID->
  "4453160f-de90-fd47-9190-b767aab890d9"],
 ", the radial acceleration is"
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.975607762347122*^9, 3.9756079594051456`*^9}, {
   3.9761346593223705`*^9, 3.976134659342066*^9}, 3.9769002955223255`*^9, {
   3.976905452370348*^9, 3.976905461339903*^9}, 3.976905700057436*^9, {
   3.976908445787344*^9, 3.9769084462654037`*^9}, {3.9769085789664173`*^9, 
   3.976908579524393*^9}, {3.9769110066838303`*^9, 3.976911007562351*^9}, 
   3.9769117780174465`*^9, {3.9771382216735897`*^9, 3.9771382626155357`*^9}, {
   3.9792341813621464`*^9, 3.979234182383524*^9}, {3.9795608377364883`*^9, 
   3.979560838722172*^9}, 3.986552831245846*^9, {3.986554641940113*^9, 
   3.986554656326975*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a6ecdbfa-9d99-5441-b994-\
1c308f40dae4"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   InterpretationBox[
    SubscriptBox["a", "in"],
    $CellContext`inwardAcceleration], "[", "r_", "]"}], "=", 
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{"-", "A"}], "-", 
   FractionBox[
    RowBox[{"G", " ", 
     RowBox[{"M", "[", "r", "]"}]}], 
    SuperscriptBox["r", "2"]]}]}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777621265832977`*^9, 3.977762198676071*^9}, {
  3.9777625344269733`*^9, 3.9777625359150524`*^9}},
 CellLabel->"In[71]:=",ExpressionUUID->"0f8c1b6d-c9a3-4a36-981d-ad2a2e0ccede"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{"-", "A"}], "-", 
  FractionBox[
   RowBox[{"G", " ", 
    RowBox[{"M", "[", "r", "]"}]}], 
   SuperscriptBox["r", "2"]]}]], "Output",
 CellChangeTimes->{3.986820802793825*^9, 3.989149847777441*^9},
 CellLabel->"Out[71]=",ExpressionUUID->"d6223c0a-63da-9246-8fc4-eb0489f3dd4e"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell["The radial acceleration of a circular orbit is:", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.986565416501835*^9, 3.986565427843878*^9}, {
  3.9865657161988945`*^9, 
  3.9865657170802937`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"8631a3e0-0c18-e345-af78-\
19782e6f6772"],

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    InterpretationBox[
     SubscriptBox["a", "cent"],
     $CellContext`centrifugalAcceleration], "[", "r_", "]"}], "=", 
   RowBox[{"-", 
    FractionBox[
     SuperscriptBox["v", "2"], "r"]}]}], ";"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.977764493666857*^9, 3.977764522903763*^9}, {
   3.977764640374361*^9, 3.977764644616535*^9}, 3.986565359306633*^9},
 CellLabel->"In[72]:=",ExpressionUUID->"87ef652a-626b-6b47-9915-54a74e74468d"],

Cell["\<\
The condition for circular motion then requires that this radial acceleration \
provide the centripetal acceleration,\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{
  3.9865538425186443`*^9, {3.986565442035389*^9, 3.9865654425189476`*^9}, {
   3.986565727686451*^9, 
   3.986565740895235*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"0e8b4056-d4ff-7947-9bb1-\
ab2a233f36c2"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell[BoxData[
 RowBox[{
  RowBox[{
   RowBox[{
    InterpretationBox[
     SubscriptBox["a", "cent"],
     $CellContext`centrifugalAcceleration], "[", "r", "]"}], "==", 
   RowBox[{
    InterpretationBox[
     SubscriptBox["a", "in"],
     $CellContext`inwardAcceleration], "[", "r", "]"}]}], "//", 
  "Simplify"}]], "Input",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9777512406128235`*^9, 3.977751259362673*^9}, 
   3.986565258549589*^9, {3.986565371343794*^9, 3.986565381277876*^9}, {
